      "source_example_id": "e-14720",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph",
      "kp_id": "e-14720"
    },
    "q-110128": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110128",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156042/assets/89dee9fffb22501189ac9f972dc4bcf7e2ed3602ef000b8ef01f04ddccd2e2eb.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $(3,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,6]$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
